ite's mirror image.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.\n- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.\n\nRule:\n- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.\n- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.\n- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.\n\nKey insight:\n- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"8-connected non-square foreground sprite s\",\n  \"dst\": \"solid 2x2 square anchor d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s faces d by cardinal separation with overlapping span\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate s to touch d's facing edge, mirror s across that edge in d.color, and clear originals\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc4aaf52\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3770\nmechanism=0.4747\nlexical=0.0141\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:draw_line, operation:reflect, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/fc4aaf52.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/fc4aaf52.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSplit the two-color object horizontally, swap its colors, and slide the upper half to the right.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- All foreground pixels form one 4-connected object with an even-height bbox.\n- The object has foreground pixels on both rows adjacent to its horizontal midpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Split the object into upper and lower halves at the midpoint of its bbox.\n- Swap the two foreground colors in both halves.\n- Keep the lower half in place and shift the upper half right until its lower-left\n  boundary pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's upper-right boundary pixel.\n\nKey insight:\n- The object is not copied or reflected; it is broken at the horizontal waist,\n  recolored by exchanging the two visible object colors, then rejoined diagonally.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"upper half s of the single two-color foreground object split at its horizontal midpoint\",\n  \"path\": \"translate s right until its bottom-row leftmost foreground pixel is diagonally adjacent to the lower half's top-row rightmost foreground pixel\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"place only the final translated upper-half sprite; leave no trail\",\n  \"dst\": \"endpoint beside the fixed lower half\",\n  \"transform\": \"exchange the two foreground colors in both halves; keep the lower half in place and clear the original upper-half cells\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3508\nmechanism=0.4360\nlexical=0.0143\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:sprite, operation:draw_line, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/465b7d93.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/465b7d93.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"small colored sprite s outside the frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"hollow rectangular frame interior d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"unique s maps to unique d; s.bbox extremes align to d.bbox extremes\",\n  \"transform\": \"clear s; stretch s into d by mapping bbox extremes, drawing 4-adjacent segments, and filling enclosed regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8dab14c2\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3251\nmechanism=0.4235\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:between, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/8dab14c2.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8dab14c2.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nComplete each boundary tab or notch by adding the complementary feature on the opposite side of the foreground object.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has exactly two colors: a dominant background and one foreground object.\n- The foreground object is a thick rectilinear shape with one-pixel tabs and notches on its boundary.\n\nRule\n- A foreground tab is a one-neighbor boundary pixel; project through its full row or column span and remove the opposite edge pixel to make a matching notch.\n- A background notch is a one-pixel cut with three foreground 4-neighbors; project across the full row or column span and add a tab just outside the opposite edge.\n- Notches immediately beside a tab source are not independent sources, because that gap belongs to the tab's local boundary shape.\n\nKey insight\n- The changed pixels are not color-specific noise; they are paired jigsaw-like boundary features on opposite sides of the same thick row or column cross-section.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"one thick monochrome foreground object on a dominant background, with one-pixel boundary tabs and missing edge pixels\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each foreground tab, remove the opposite edge pixel on the same row or column; for each missing edge pixel, add a foreground tab just outside the opposite edge, ignoring gaps immediately beside a tab source\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3417\nmechanism=0.4226\nlexical=0.0144\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:draw_line, operation:reflect, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"825aa9e9\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3554\nmechanism=0.4598\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:grid_edge, object:sprite, operation:draw_line, operation:translate, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/825aa9e9.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/825aa9e9.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nDrop the moving-color objects downward until they rest above a one-row cushion before the obstacle or bottom edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has exactly three colors: empty background, a stationary obstacle/floor color, and a moving object color.\n- The obstacle color is the unique non-background role that touches the bottom edge.\n- Moving pixels start outside the obstacle cells, outside the row directly above each obstacle pixel, and outside the bottom row.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background and obstacle pixels fixed.\n- Treat obstacle pixels, cells directly above obstacle pixels, and the bottom row as blocked.\n- Repeatedly move every 4-connected moving-color object one row down when its translated shape does not hit blocked cells or another moving object.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
